PLANET-SCAN: A Decision-Time-Constrained Framework for Independent Spacecraft Telemetry-Fidelity Assessment Through External Physical Observation

Fourth Revised Theoretical Draft — Version 0.5

Author: Ouadi Maakoul
Research Collaboration: SI TEAM
Date: November 15, 2026

Research status: Theoretical architecture specification. No demonstrated system, validated performance, or established novelty claim. Every quantitative figure cited from prior work is attributed to its source; no figure is transferred to lunar or Martian conditions. Version 0.5 responds to a structured verification of the Version 0.4 reference base and to a technical critique of the credibility-weighted machinery. Five substantive changes are made: the credible-set construction is rendered symmetric so that subsets excluding suspect external modalities are admissible; the credibility layer is given a Bayesian formulation with an explicit prior on the Byzantine fraction, and the heuristic argmin of Version 0.4 is demoted to an initializer; the degenerate single-suspect-channel case is stated explicitly and its breakdown point is derived; the equivalence test is corrected to a two-one-sided-test formulation with a pre-specified primary comparator; and the adversarial-consistency corruption class is reclassified from a falsification arm to a robustness check. The truncated-SPRT citation is corrected, the unreferenced thermography figures are removed, and the observability-demand audit—absent in Versions 0.3 and 0.4—is added. The circularity problem remains unresolved; its treatment is now expressed as a subset-existence condition whose failure mode is explicit abstention.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 The diagnostic reliability problem

Future lunar and Martian transportation systems will depend on reusable vehicles whose condition must be assessed rapidly during approach and descent. Contemporary spacecraft health monitoring relies on embedded instrumentation, onboard diagnostic software, and ground-based processing. These systems are indispensable, but their evidentiary value is bounded by a structural vulnerability: the diagnostic pathway is generally internal to the vehicle whose condition it is assessing.

The threat model is documented. Vanlyssel et al. demonstrate, in NASA's NOS3 simulation environment, a supply-chain implant that produces telemetry packets preserving expected formatting and cadence while misleading ground-side estimators and operators. The demonstration establishes plausibility, not prevalence. The relevance to PLANET-SCAN is that schema-valid, cadence-correct telemetry can pass conventional acceptance checks while carrying false physical information. The problem is therefore not merely physical spacecraft damage. It is a problem of evidentiary independence: the information used to certify the vehicle may share failure modes with the vehicle itself.

1.2 The independent-witness concept and its two-sided limit

PLANET-SCAN investigates whether an external observation pathway can provide statistically meaningful evidence that the vehicle's declared physical state is inconsistent with its observable behavior. The distinction from ordinary sensor fusion is deliberate: sensor fusion improves a state estimate, whereas PLANET-SCAN tests a hypothesis about the fidelity of the telemetry channel itself.

A critical boundary must be stated at the outset, and it is two-sided. The external channel is not ground truth. Infrared thermography retrieval carries systematic uncertainty from emissivity, transmittance, and calibration assumptions, and those uncertainties are state-dependent and model-mediated. The external measurement is a model-based inference, and the model may share assumptions with the telemetry-derived state. But the converse error is equally fatal: if the external modalities themselves share a systematic bias—precisely the case that a factor-analytic cross-covariance model is built to represent—then faithful telemetry will be scored as non-credible, and the architecture will certify corruption when the external majority is wrong. The credible-set construction of Section 3 is therefore symmetric: subsets that exclude suspect external modalities are as admissible as subsets that exclude telemetry, and external systematic error is a first-class diagnostic outcome, not merely a residual-layer nuisance.

1.3 What Version 0.5 changes

Version 0.4 replaced binary independence with a credibility-weighted joint estimation layer, but the replacement introduced a silent assumption: that the mutually consistent external modalities define truth. That is the mirror image of the circularity Version 0.4 was designed to escape. Version 0.5 corrects the asymmetry, states the degenerate single-suspect case explicitly, derives the consistency-vote breakdown point, and replaces the formal-looking but under-specified argmin with a Bayesian posterior over state and credibility. It also corrects the falsification logic: the adversarial-consistency arm of Version 0.4 could not fail because the corruption was constructed to be invisible to the telemetry-only detector. That arm is now a robustness check with a predicted win; the falsification arena is the natural fault classes, where a null result is possible.

The revisions are organized as follows. Section 2 restates the formal problem with the separability condition and the symmetric hypothesis family. Section 3 specifies the Bayesian credibility layer and the breakdown point of the single-suspect consistency vote. Section 4 specifies the factor-analytic cross-covariance structure and the pre-flight estimation requirement. Section 5 specifies the decision layer with boundary-crossing probabilities and Siegmund-corrected approximations. Section 6 specifies deadline-constrained value of information with an exogenous decision-authority hazard. Section 7 supplies the observability-demand audit. Section 8 states the corrected falsification strategy. Section 9 states the open problems, with the subset-existence condition as the primary unresolved constraint.

