2. Formal Problem Statement

2.1 The decision-relevant detection guarantee

Let H_0 denote faithful telemetry and H_1 a specified class of telemetry-inconsistency. Let d \in \{H_0, H_1, \mathrm{ABSTAIN}\} be the terminal decision, \tau the stopping time, and T_{\mathrm{dec}} the deadline before an irreversible action. The requirement is:

\Pr(d = H_1 \mid H_1) \ge 1 - \beta, \qquad
\Pr(d = H_1 \mid H_0) \le \alpha, \qquad
\tau \le T_{\mathrm{dec}},

where \alpha is the false-alarm probability and \beta the missed-detection probability. The truncated-test literature establishes that a finite deadline changes the achievable operating point. Golhar and Pollock show that truncation must be compensated by choosing truncation points such that the actual error probabilities of the truncated test are no worse than the desired probabilities, using Wald's constant bounds as the conservative envelope. The design must therefore choose thresholds for the truncated procedure, not for the asymptotic procedure.

2.2 The hypothesis family and the separability condition

Let \theta be a low-dimensional discrepancy vector—an external-temperature bias, an attitude error, a propulsion-performance deviation—and let \eta collect external model nuisance parameters: emissivity, transmittance, calibration state, viewing geometry. The hypotheses are

H_0: \theta = \theta_0, \qquad
H_{1,j}: \theta \in \Theta_j \subset \Theta \setminus \{\theta_0\}, \quad j = 1, \dots, J.

Not every j is testable at the achievable external information level. Define the separability margin

\Delta_j \;=\; \lambda_{\min}\!\left(
\mathcal{I}_{\theta\theta}(\theta_0) -
\mathcal{I}_{\theta\eta}(\theta_0)\,
\mathcal{I}_{\eta\eta}(\theta_0)^{-1}\,
\mathcal{I}_{\eta\theta}(\theta_0)
\right),

where \mathcal{I} is the joint Fisher information for (\theta, \eta). The separability condition is \Delta_j > 0. This Schur-complement construction is the standard local-identifiability criterion under nuisance parameters. When \Delta_j \le 0, the signal lies in a calibration direction and the hypothesis is not physically identifiable, regardless of sample size. Hypotheses violating \Delta_j > 0 are removed from the testable family, or the external-sensing requirement is strengthened until the condition holds, or the hypothesis is declared externally untestable. The condition is necessary but not sufficient: \Delta_j > 0 establishes local identifiability, not finite-sample detectability under T_{\mathrm{dec}}; the latter is supplied by the boundary-crossing surface of Section 5.

2.3 The symmetric diagnostic posterior

A discrepancy between telemetry and external observation does not by itself imply telemetry corruption. It may arise from sensor failure, telemetry substitution, forward-model mismatch, external measurement error, or a true physical anomaly. The architecture produces a discrete posterior over diagnostic classes:

\Pr(M = m \mid z^{\mathrm{ext}}_{1:k}, z^{\mathrm{int}}_{1:k}, c_{1:k}),
\qquad
m \in \mathcal{M} = \{\text{faithful},\ \text{sensor fault},\ \text{telemetry corruption},\ \text{model mismatch},\ \text{external systematic error}\},

where c_k \in [0,1] is the latent credibility of the telemetry channel at time k. The class external systematic error is the explicit outcome that Version 0.4 lacked: a configuration in which the external modalities are mutually consistent but jointly biased, and the telemetry is faithful. Without this class, the posterior cannot represent the inversion failure mode described in Section 1.2.

