4. Factor-Analytic Cross-Covariance Structure

4.1 The \beta-exponent is the wrong object

Version 0.3 proposed \Lambda_k^{\mathrm{eff}} = \Lambda_k^{1-\beta_{\mathrm{ind}}}. The common-cause \beta-factor is a static, component-level fraction of failures that are common-cause; it is not a time-varying correlation parameter between two measurement channels. Applying it to modulate a sequential likelihood ratio conflates failure-event correlation with measurement-error correlation. The correct object is the joint distribution p(z_k^{\mathrm{ext}}, z_k^{\mathrm{int}} \mid x_k, H_i), whose deviation from conditional independence is a covariance structure.

4.2 The factor model and its physical interpretation

Let \tilde{e}_k^{(w)} = \Sigma_w^{-1/2}(z_k^{(w)} - h^{(w)}(x_k)) be the standardized measurement errors. The conditional-independence null is \mathrm{Cov}(\tilde{e}_k^{(0)}, \tilde{e}_k^{(w)}) = 0 for all external w \ge 1. A tractable alternative is the factor-analytic shared-error model:

\tilde{e}_k^{(w)} = \Lambda_w f_k + u_k^{(w)}, \qquad
f_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I_r), \quad u_k^{(w)} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Psi_w),

where f_k \in \mathbb{R}^r is a vector of latent common-mode variables with a named physical interpretation: shared atmospheric density estimate, shared clock bias, shared thermal-model prior, and shared ephemeris product. The loading \Lambda_w expresses how strongly modality w inherits each common-mode error, and \Psi_w is the modality-specific residual covariance. The cross-covariance is \Sigma_{\mathrm{cross}}^{(0,w)} = \Lambda_0 \Lambda_w^\top, and conditional independence holds if and only if \Lambda_0 \Lambda_w^\top = 0 for all w. Because factor rotation is indeterminate, the restriction is testable only when the latent dimension r and the sparsity pattern of the loadings are constrained by physics; the paper therefore commits to r \le 3 with the named common modes above and imposes zero loadings for modalities that are physically insulated from a given mode (for example, radar range is not loaded on the thermal-model prior).

The residual covariance in Layer 2 becomes

\Sigma_r = \Sigma_{\mathrm{ext}} + H_{\mathrm{ext}} P_k^{(-0)} H_{\mathrm{ext}}^\top + \Lambda_0 \Lambda_{\mathrm{ext}}^\top + \Lambda_{\mathrm{ext}} \Lambda_0^\top,

which is valid under Gaussian additive errors; the cross terms are linear because the factor model is additive in f_k. The sensitivity analysis varies the eigenstructure of \Lambda_0 \Lambda_{\mathrm{ext}}^\top, not a scalar.

4.3 Pre-flight estimation and in-descent testing

A single descent provides too few samples for factor goodness-of-fit, and the honest specification is that \Lambda is estimated from pre-flight calibration data—ground tests, thermal-vacuum campaigns, and high-fidelity simulation—and held fixed during descent. The descent data test only the conditional-independence restriction \Lambda_0 \Lambda_{\mathrm{ext}}^\top = 0 and the plausibility of the pre-flight loadings. If the in-descent residual covariance is inconsistent with the pre-flight \Lambda, the architecture treats that inconsistency as evidence of an unmodeled common mode and raises the abstention probability; it does not attempt to re-estimate \Lambda online from insufficient data.

