5. Decision Layer: Boundary-Crossing Probabilities and Corrected Approximations

5.1 From Fisher information to the truncated-test observable

The Fisher information matrix measures asymptotic estimation precision, not finite-sample sequential detection power under a deadline. A high FIM during powered descent does not guarantee that the likelihood-ratio trajectory will cross the SPRT boundary before T_{\mathrm{dec}}. The decision-relevant observable is the boundary-crossing probability

P_{\mathrm{det}}(T_{\mathrm{dec}} \mid \theta_j) =
\Pr\!\left( \exists\, k \le n(T_{\mathrm{dec}}) : \Lambda_k \ge B \;\middle|\; H_{1,j} \right),

or the equivalent CUSUM crossing probability. The FIM and expected KL divergence are retained as screening tools to identify which signatures merit simulation; the operational design output is the boundary-crossing surface P_{\mathrm{det}}(T_{\mathrm{dec}} \mid \theta_j, \text{cadence}, \Sigma_{\mathrm{ext}}, \Lambda).

5.2 Corrected diffusion approximations

At the small \alpha relevant to flight-safety decisions, uncorrected Brownian-motion approximations to the boundary-crossing probability can be off by tens of percent, and the whole design object is that probability. Siegmund’s corrected diffusion approximations incorporate the overshoot of the test statistic past the boundary and are typically as good as, and often better than, large-deviation approximations for linear boundaries, including truncated sequential probability ratio tests. The boundary-crossing surface is therefore computed with Siegmund-corrected approximations or exact recursions where feasible, not with the uncorrected Brownian approximation.

5.3 CUSUM, SPRT, and the test-statistic ablation

CUSUM is appropriate for unknown onset:

S_k = \max(0, S_{k-1} + \ell_k - \nu), \qquad d = H_1 \text{ when } S_k > h.

SPRT is the baseline for two simple hypotheses under i.i.d. observations:

\Lambda_k = \sum_{i=1}^{k} \log \frac{p(z_i \mid H_1)}{p(z_i \mid H_0)},
\qquad \text{stop when } \Lambda_k \notin (A, B).

Neither is universally optimal; the debris-strike telemetry study implements CUSUM, SPRT, and Shiryaev SPRT as alternatives precisely because relative performance depends on fault magnitude and noise regime. The architecture reports the boundary-crossing surface for each statistic, and the falsification strategy includes a test-statistic ablation (Section 8).

5.4 Abstention

The decision set is d \in \{H_0, H_1, \mathrm{ABSTAIN}\}. Abstention is required when \mathcal{C}_\delta = \emptyset (no credible subset) or when \Delta_j \le 0 for all testable j (no separable hypothesis). An architecture that cannot abstain will produce a decision under conditions where the evidence structure does not support one.

