6. Deadline-Constrained Value of Information

6.1 Exogenous loss of optionality versus operator-controlled commit

The decision-authority hazard h_{\mathrm{DA}}(t) must not double-count the deadline already imposed by \tau \le T_{\mathrm{dec}}. Two distinct mechanisms close the decision option: exogenous loss of optionality—fuel depletion, terrain commit points, communication blackout, aerodynamic limits—which the operator does not control, and decision-maker-controlled commit, which is the operator’s own stopping boundary. Only the exogenous component belongs in h_{\mathrm{DA}}(t); the controlled component is already represented in the sequential stopping rule. Writing h_{\mathrm{DA}}(t) = h_{\mathrm{exo}}(t) and excluding the operator’s commit policy from the hazard preserves the distinction.

6.2 The deadline-constrained value of information

Define the exogenous survival function

S_{\mathrm{exo}}(t) = \exp\!\left(-\int_0^t h_{\mathrm{exo}}(s)\,ds\right).

The deadline-constrained value of an observation at time t with processing delay \delta is

\mathrm{VoI}_{\mathrm{DC}}(t) =
S_{\mathrm{exo}}(t+\delta)\,
\mathbb{E}_{z_t}\!\left[
\max_{d} \mathbb{E}[U(d,F) \mid \mathcal{F}_t, z_t]
-
\max_{d} \mathbb{E}[U(d,F) \mid \mathcal{F}_t]
\right],

where U(d,F) is the payoff of decision d under true fidelity state F, and \mathcal{F}_t is the filtration generated by observations up to t. The factor S_{\mathrm{exo}}(t+\delta) is the probability that the decision option survives until the observation is usable. An observation with S_{\mathrm{exo}}(t+\delta) \approx 0 has near-zero decision value even if its statistical likelihood ratio is large. The Lehrer–Wang stopping-problem framework establishes that the upfront fee scheme is optimal among history-dependent schemes and that more accurate sources and higher discount factors both enlarge the waiting region; the exogenous hazard is the physical analogue of the discount factor.

6.3 The constrained cost minimization

The decision rule is

\min_{\pi} \; \mathbb{E}\!\left[ C_{\mathrm{miss}}\mathbf{1}_{\mathrm{miss}}
+ C_{\mathrm{FA}}\mathbf{1}_{\mathrm{false\ alarm}}
+ C_{\mathrm{delay}}\tau
+ C_{\mathrm{obs}}N
+ C_{\mathrm{abs}}\mathbf{1}_{\mathrm{ABSTAIN}} \right]

subject to \Pr(\text{false alarm}) \le \alpha, \Pr(\text{missed detection}) \le \beta, and \tau \le T_{\mathrm{dec}}. The abstention cost C_{\mathrm{abs}} makes the no-credible-subset case explicit in the objective. The costs are asymmetric and mission-specific.

