7. Observability-Demand Audit

7.1 The missing demand side

Versions 0.3 and 0.4 specified the supply side—what external modalities can observe—without confronting the demand side: which declared telemetry quantities have any external forward model at all. Most safety-relevant channels have none. Tank pressures, valve states, battery health, avionics bus voltages, and software fault flags are internal quantities with no external physical signature that an airborne or orbital sensor can reconstruct. The architecture cannot test the fidelity of a channel whose physical consequences are not externally observable, and no amount of modality-portfolio optimization changes that.

7.2 The audit table

The paper therefore requires an explicit audit. For each declared telemetry channel i, record:

1. whether a forward model h_{\mathrm{ext}}^{(i)}(x) exists that maps the physical state to an external observable;
2. the signature strength, expressed as the separability margin \Delta_j^{(i)} of Section 2.2 under each candidate modality;
3. the phase window in which the signature is observable;
4. whether the channel is externally testable, externally testable only in combination with another channel, or externally untestable.

A representative audit, stated qualitatively because the quantitative margins require the physical analysis identified in Section 9.2:

· Surface temperature and thermal-map structure: externally testable by IR thermography, with \Delta_j conditional on emissivity and transmittance priors; the HYTHIRM observational envelope (airborne Cast Glance imaging at 26–41 nautical miles, Mach 8.4 and 14.7 measurements, MODTRAN atmospheric correction, emissivity adjustment, frame averaging) bounds what has been demonstrated, not what is achievable at Mars or the Moon.
· Attitude and spin state: externally testable by visible-band photometry, where light-curve inversion recovers attitude for unresolved resident space objects and ground-based photometry determines spacecraft attitude through light-curve inversion; the signature is strong but shared loading on the ephemeris product must be modeled.
· Position and range: externally testable by radar tracking and by external navigation fixes; radar is already part of the descent navigation suite, and DSN ranging combined with optical and cooperative radar measurements provides position knowledge independent of onboard state estimation.
· Propulsion performance: externally testable only through its integrated effect on the trajectory, which couples to the navigation solution and therefore carries shared loading on the clock and ephemeris factors; the separability margin is correspondingly fragile.
· Tank pressure, valve state, battery health, avionics bus voltage, software fault flags: externally untestable. No forward model maps these quantities to an external observable at the required precision. For these channels, PLANET-SCAN offers no fidelity evidence, and the architecture must say so rather than implying uniform coverage.

The audit is the single highest-value addition for external readers because it converts the framework’s scope claim from an implicit universal to an explicit partition: some channels are externally witnessable, some are only witnessable in combination, and some are not witnessable at all.

