8. Falsification Strategy

8.1 The corrected equivalence test

Version 0.4 stated the null as H_0^{\mathrm{equiv}}: P_{\mathrm{det}}^{\mathrm{PS}} - P_{\mathrm{det}}^{\mathrm{best}} \le \Delta, which is a superiority hypothesis: rejecting it shows PLANET-SCAN is better by more than \Delta, but failing to reject it does not establish equivalence. Version 0.5 replaces this with a two-one-sided-test (TOST) formulation on the absolute difference:

H_0^{\mathrm{TOST}}: \left| P_{\mathrm{det}}^{\mathrm{PS}}(T_{\mathrm{dec}}) - P_{\mathrm{det}}^{\mathrm{comp}}(T_{\mathrm{dec}}) \right| \ge \Delta
\quad \text{versus} \quad
H_1^{\mathrm{TOST}}: \left| P_{\mathrm{det}}^{\mathrm{PS}} - P_{\mathrm{det}}^{\mathrm{comp}} \right| < \Delta,

tested at level \alpha_{\mathrm{equiv}} on each side. Rejecting both one-sided nulls establishes equivalence within the margin \Delta. Failing to reject does not establish equivalence and must not be reported as if it did. The margin \Delta is pre-specified from mission risk tolerance, not statistical convention.

The primary comparator is pre-specified as the transformer-based telemetry-only detector (HPM-Former or equivalent), not the post-hoc “best single layer,” because post-hoc selection injects winner’s-curse bias into the primary falsification claim. Secondary comparisons against the Bayesian filter, the residual threshold test, and SPRT/CUSUM are reported as secondary and are not used to establish or refute the architecture’s added value. All comparisons are made at matched \alpha: detection probabilities at different false-alarm rates are not comparable.

The transformer baseline is used with two disclosed caveats. It is a health-state anomaly detector, not a fidelity detector, so it measures anomaly-detection capability rather than fidelity certification; and it trains partly on synthetic sustained-offset samples derived from nominal telemetry, which may make it stronger than an operational detector trained on real fault data. Both caveats cut in the direction of a conservative falsification test: if PLANET-SCAN cannot outperform a baseline with these advantages, the case for the architecture is weak.

8.2 Natural fault classes as the falsification arena

The informative comparison is on natural fault classes—sensor drift, calibration bias, true physical anomalies, model mismatch—where the added value of an expensive external channel is genuinely in doubt and a null result is possible. These are the classes on which the TOST is run. A failure to reject equivalence on the natural classes is a substantive negative result about the architecture, not a tuning artifact.

8.3 Adversarial consistency as a robustness check

The adversarial-consistency corruption class of Section 3.4 is constructed to minimize the telemetry-only detector’s likelihood ratio while displacing the physical state. A telemetry-only detector is structurally blind to this class by design, so PLANET-SCAN will trivially “win” the comparison. That arm therefore cannot falsify anything, and Version 0.5 reclassifies it as a robustness check with a predicted win: if the external channel does not detect a representationally consistent corruption, the architecture has failed even on its home ground, and that is an alarming result rather than a routine negative one.

8.4 The falsification sequence

1. Select three to five natural corruption classes with distinct separability profiles and physically interpretable external signatures.
2. Construct a generative simulator with separate true-state, onboard-sensor, telemetry-corruption (including adversarial consistency), external-observation, and decision modules.
3. For each class, compute the separability margin \Delta_j under each candidate modality portfolio; exclude classes with \Delta_j \le 0 from the testable family.
4. Estimate the factor loadings \Lambda from pre-flight calibration data; test the conditional-independence restriction on descent data.
5. Compute boundary-crossing surfaces P_{\mathrm{det}}(T_{\mathrm{dec}} \mid \theta_j) with Siegmund-corrected approximations for each test statistic and each baseline.
6. Evaluate the deadline-constrained VoI under the exogenous decision-authority hazard h_{\mathrm{exo}}(t).
7. Perform sensitivity analysis on emissivity, transmittance, calibration bias, and the eigenstructure of \Lambda_0 \Lambda_{\mathrm{ext}}^\top.
8. Run the TOST against the pre-specified transformer comparator at matched \alpha, with the pre-registered margin \Delta.
9. Report the minimum external-sensing requirement for a specified (\alpha, \beta, T_{\mathrm{dec}}, \Delta) operating point, or the subset-existence condition under which no such requirement exists.

A negative result at step 3 or step 9 bounds the conditions under which the architecture is useful. A failure to reject equivalence at step 8 is a substantive negative result about the architecture’s added value. The adversarial-consistency robustness check at step 2 is reported separately and is not used to establish superiority.

