9. Open Problems

9.1 The subset-existence condition

The credibility-weighted formulation replaces binary independence with a subset-existence condition: the fidelity test is defined only when at least one credible subset \mathcal{S} \in \mathcal{C}_\delta excludes the telemetry channel. Whether such a subset exists under realistic Byzantine fractions and measurement models is an open empirical question. The breakdown-point analysis of Section 3.2 gives a necessary condition—at least three external modalities with distinct nuisance structures—but not a sufficient one, because the shared-loading structure of Section 4 can make three externals behave as fewer than three independent witnesses. If the Byzantine fraction exceeds the effective breakdown point, the architecture must abstain, and the external observation provides no decision-relevant evidence before T_{\mathrm{dec}}. This is the primary mathematical obstacle.

9.2 Separability under realistic planetary nuisance structure

The separability margin \Delta_j depends on the joint information \mathcal{I}(\theta, \eta). The nuisance structure \eta for Martian and lunar conditions—emissivity, atmospheric opacity, illumination geometry, calibration stability—is not established at the precision required to certify \Delta_j > 0 for the corruption classes of interest. This requires a separate physical analysis, and the audit of Section 7.2 is qualitative until that analysis is performed.

9.3 Identifiability of the factor loadings

The restriction \Lambda_0 \Lambda_{\mathrm{ext}}^\top = 0 is testable only if r and the loading sparsity pattern are constrained by physics. Factor rotation indeterminacy means that many \Lambda fit the same covariances, and a vanishing tetrad constraint can rule out some covariance structures but does not uniquely establish a common factor. The physical naming of the common modes in Section 4.2 is a commitment that must be validated against pre-flight calibration data.

9.4 Operational deadline-constrained VoI

The expression is conceptually clear, but h_{\mathrm{exo}}(t) is mission- and phase-specific and must be derived from the guidance, navigation, and control timeline. Separating exogenous loss of optionality from operator-controlled commit is necessary to avoid double-counting, but the exogenous component itself is not trivially observable and may require coupled trajectory–propellant simulation.

9.5 Planetary transferability of the modality portfolio

No source establishes that HYTHIRM-level thermography or light-curve attitude inversion at the required cadence is achievable at Mars or on the lunar surface with the separability margins the architecture requires. The portfolio optimization is a design framework, not a demonstrated capability.

9.6 What would constitute progress

Progress on the subset-existence condition would be a theorem or a counterexample: a class of measurement models and Byzantine fractions for which a credible subset excluding telemetry exists with probability at least 1-\delta, or a demonstration that for plausible descent measurement models the condition fails. Progress on separability would be a phase-dependent map of \Delta_j for the candidate modalities, identifying which channels are externally testable and which are not—the quantitative completion of the audit in Section 7. Progress on the falsification strategy would be a TOST result against the pre-specified transformer comparator at matched \alpha, with the pre-registered margin \Delta. A well-powered failure to reject equivalence would be an informative negative result, not a null outcome.

---

10. Conclusion

PLANET-SCAN Version 0.5 is a more symmetric and more falsifiable specification than Version 0.4. It no longer treats the external majority as the definition of truth: the credible set admits subsets excluding suspect externals, the diagnostic posterior includes external systematic error as a first-class outcome, and the breakdown-point analysis states plainly what a consistency vote over W externals can and cannot adjudicate. It replaces the under-specified credibility argmin with a Bayesian posterior over state, Byzantine indicators, and Byzantine fraction, with the \beta-factor literature supplying the prior scale. It states that the factor loadings are estimated pre-flight and held fixed, and that factor rotation requires physical naming of the common modes. It corrects the truncated-test citation to Golhar and Pollock, restores the unreferenced thermography figures to a properly attributed qualitative statement, and adds the observability-demand audit that Versions 0.3 and 0.4 lacked. It corrects the equivalence test to TOST with a pre-specified comparator and matched \alpha, and it reclassifies the adversarial-consistency class from a falsification arm to a robustness check with a predicted win.

The central unresolved problem is the subset-existence condition: whether a credible evidence subset excluding the telemetry channel exists with sufficient probability before the exogenous decision-authority hazard closes the option, given the shared-loading structure among the external modalities. The breakdown point f^{*} = \lfloor (W-1)/2 \rfloor is a necessary condition, not a sufficient one. Until the condition is resolved—either by a theorem for a class of measurement models or by a counterexample that bounds the architecture’s applicability—the framework’s independence claim remains conditional. The honest research path is to bound that condition through separability analysis, factor-analytic covariance testing, and a well-powered TOST against the strongest telemetry-only baseline, before claiming operational utility.

