Appendix A — Derivation of the Consistency-Vote Breakdown Point

A.1 Object

This appendix derives the breakdown point f^{*} of the symmetric credibility layer of Section 3.2 under the single-suspect configuration: one telemetry channel (source 0) and W external modalities (sources 1,\dots,W), with no inter-agent measurement graph among the externals. It makes precise the sense in which the Byzantine credible-subset formalism of Holden et al. transfers to the single-vehicle case, and the sense in which it does not. Holden et al. evaluate candidate credible rover subsets by comparing the statistical consistency of their internal and boundary relative detections against priors, and show that internally consistent Byzantine agents can make truthful incriminating measurements appear as outliers [9†L22-L27]. That structure presupposes multiple suspect agents and a relative-detection graph. The single-vehicle case has neither, so the appropriate object is a vote with a known breakdown point, not a full subset search.

A.2 The vote

Let a_w \in \{\text{consistent}, \text{inconsistent}\} be the pairwise agreement indicator between source w and the current consensus estimate. A candidate credible subset \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{W} is admissible if it contains at least \lceil (W+1)/2 \rceil external sources that are mutually consistent, plus the telemetry channel or minus it. The two admissible families are

\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{tel\ included}} = \{0\} \cup \mathcal{E}, \qquad
\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{tel\ excluded}} = \mathcal{E}',

where \mathcal{E}, \mathcal{E}' \subseteq \{1,\dots,W\} are mutually consistent external subsets. The fidelity test is defined when \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{tel\ excluded}} is non-empty and credible; the external-validation test (the symmetric check that guards against the inversion failure mode of Section 1.2) is defined when \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{tel\ included}} is credible and at least one external is excluded.

A.3 Breakdown point

Let f external modalities be jointly biased in a common direction—the shared-loading case that the factor model of Section 4 is designed to represent. A credible subset excluding the biased externals and including telemetry exists if and only if

W - f \;\ge\; \left\lceil \frac{W+1}{2} \right\rceil,

because the credible external block must outnumber the biased block. Solving for the largest admissible f,

f^{*} = W - \left\lceil \frac{W+1}{2} \right\rceil = \left\lfloor \frac{W-1}{2} \right\rfloor.

The result is the standard Byzantine majority threshold: a voting scheme tolerates fewer than half of the participants being faulty [17†L8-L9]. The values are:

W f^{*} Consequence
1 0 A single external cannot be adjudicated against telemetry; abstain.
2 0 Two externals that disagree cannot be adjudicated without a third; abstain.
3 1 One biased external can be outvoted.
4 1 Two jointly biased externals mimic two faithful externals; the vote alone cannot break the tie.
5 2 Two biased externals can be outvoted.

A.4 Design consequence

The breakdown point is a design constraint on the modality portfolio, not a property of the inference algorithm. At least three external modalities with distinct nuisance-parameter structures are required before the consistency vote has any nonzero resistance to a single shared external bias; at W=4, the vote is still fragile to a two-source shared bias, and the factor-analytic covariance of Appendix C is required to break the tie. This is the precise content of the claim in Section 3.2 that the Byzantine formalism “transfers in form but not in structure”: the form is the credible-subset consistency criterion, the structure is the relative-detection graph, and the single-vehicle case supplies the former but not the latter.

---

Appendix B — The Separability Condition and Its Role

B.1 Object

This appendix states the separability margin \Delta_j used in Sections 2.2 and 8.4, derives it as a Schur complement, and explains why it is necessary but not sufficient for finite-sample detectability under T_{\mathrm{dec}}.

B.2 Derivation

Let \theta be the discrepancy parameter of interest and \eta the external nuisance parameters (emissivity, transmittance, calibration state, viewing geometry). The joint Fisher information is

\mathcal{I}(\theta,\eta) =
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathcal{I}_{\theta\theta} & \mathcal{I}_{\theta\eta} \\
\mathcal{I}_{\eta\theta} & \mathcal{I}_{\eta\eta}
\end{pmatrix}.

The effective information about \theta after marginalizing the nuisance parameters is the Schur complement

\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{eff}}(\theta) =
\mathcal{I}_{\theta\theta} -
\mathcal{I}_{\theta\eta}\,
\mathcal{I}_{\eta\eta}^{-1}\,
\mathcal{I}_{\eta\theta}.

The separability margin is its smallest eigenvalue:

\Delta_j = \lambda_{\min}\!\left(\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{eff}}(\theta_0)\right).

The condition \Delta_j > 0 is equivalent to local identifiability of \theta in the presence of \eta: if \Delta_j \le 0, there exists a direction in parameter space along which \theta can be displaced while compensating with a change in \eta at no cost in likelihood. A large raw sensitivity \mathcal{I}_{\theta\theta} does not imply identifiability, because the signal may lie in a calibration direction that \mathcal{I}_{\theta\eta} can absorb. This is the formal content of the warning in Section 2.2 that a large raw sensitivity does not imply physical identifiability.

B.3 Necessity versus sufficiency

\Delta_j > 0 establishes local identifiability. It does not establish finite-sample detectability under T_{\mathrm{dec}}: a parameter can be locally identifiable with an information level so low that the boundary-crossing probability at the deadline is negligible. The finite-sample question is answered by the boundary-crossing surface of Appendix D, and the two conditions are logically independent. A hypothesis that passes \Delta_j > 0 but fails the boundary-crossing test is identifiable but undetectable in time; a hypothesis that passes the boundary-crossing test but fails \Delta_j > 0 is detectable in principle only by confounding it with calibration, which is not a fidelity test. Both failures are informative and both are reported separately in the falsification sequence of Section 8.4.

---

Appendix C — Factor-Analytic Cross-Covariance: Identification and Rotation Constraints

C.1 Object

This appendix formalizes the shared-error model of Section 4.2, states the rotation indeterminacy that makes the conditional-independence restriction weakly identified without physical constraints, and specifies the sparsity pattern that restores testability.

C.2 The model

Let \tilde{e}_k^{(w)} = \Sigma_w^{-1/2}(z_k^{(w)} - h^{(w)}(x_k)) be standardized errors. The factor model is

\tilde{e}_k^{(w)} = \Lambda_w f_k + u_k^{(w)}, \qquad
f_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I_r), \quad u_k^{(w)} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Psi_w),

with cross-covariance \Sigma_{\mathrm{cross}}^{(0,w)} = \Lambda_0 \Lambda_w^\top. Conditional independence holds if and only if \Lambda_0 \Lambda_w^\top = 0 for all w \ge 1. The covariance contribution to the Layer 2 residual is linear,

\Sigma_r = \Sigma_{\mathrm{ext}} + H_{\mathrm{ext}} P_k^{(-0)} H_{\mathrm{ext}}^\top
+ \Lambda_0 \Lambda_{\mathrm{ext}}^\top + \Lambda_{\mathrm{ext}} \Lambda_0^\top,

which is valid under Gaussian additive errors because the factor model is additive in f_k.

C.3 Rotation indeterminacy

For any orthogonal Q \in O(r), the transformation \Lambda_w \mapsto \Lambda_w Q, f_k \mapsto Q^\top f_k leaves the covariance \Lambda_w \Lambda_w^\top unchanged. The restriction \Lambda_0 \Lambda_{\mathrm{ext}}^\top = 0 is therefore not identified from covariance data alone unless the rotation is fixed by constraints. The paper commits to r \le 3 and names the latent variables physically:

· f^{(1)}: shared atmospheric density or opacity estimate;
· f^{(2)}: shared clock bias;
· f^{(3)}: shared thermal-model or ephemeris prior.

The naming is not decorative: it imposes a sparsity pattern on the loadings. Radar range is physically insulated from the thermal-model prior, so \Lambda_{\mathrm{radar},3} = 0; visible-band photometry is insulated from the atmospheric-density factor to first order, so \Lambda_{\mathrm{vis},1} = 0; telemetry inherits all three factors because the onboard estimator consumes the shared products. With r and the zero pattern fixed, the restriction \Lambda_0 \Lambda_{\mathrm{ext}}^\top = 0 becomes a testable over-identifying restriction, and a vanishing tetrad constraint can rule out some covariance structures even though it cannot uniquely establish a common factor.

C.4 Pre-flight estimation

A single descent provides too few samples for factor goodness-of-fit. \Lambda is estimated from pre-flight calibration—ground tests, thermal-vacuum campaigns, high-fidelity simulation—and held fixed during descent. The descent data test only the conditional-independence restriction and the plausibility of the pre-flight loadings. If the in-descent residual covariance is inconsistent with the pre-flight \Lambda, the architecture treats that inconsistency as evidence of an unmodeled common mode and raises the abstention probability; it does not attempt to re-estimate \Lambda online from insufficient data.

---

Appendix D — Truncated SPRT Compensation and Corrected Boundary-Crossing Approximations

D.1 Truncation changes the operating point

Wald's SPRT uses the boundary approximation

a \cong \ln\!\left(\frac{\beta_d}{1-\alpha_d}\right), \qquad
b \cong \ln\!\left(\frac{1-\beta_d}{\alpha_d}\right),

where \alpha_d and \beta_d are the desired error probabilities [8†L24-L28]. Truncation at m observations changes the actual error probabilities. Golhar and Pollock state the design objective precisely: choose, in advance, the smallest truncation point m such that the actual error probabilities achieved by the test are less than or equal to the desired values, while retaining Wald's easily computable bounds [8†L8-L12][8†L28-L33]. This is the correct formulation of the compensation requirement: the thresholds are chosen for the truncated procedure, not for the asymptotic procedure.

D.2 Corrected boundary-crossing probability

The boundary-crossing probability at the deadline is

P_{\mathrm{det}}(T_{\mathrm{dec}} \mid \theta_j) =
\Pr\!\left( \exists\, k \le n(T_{\mathrm{dec}}) : \Lambda_k \ge b \;\middle|\; H_{1,j} \right).

At the small \alpha relevant to flight-safety decisions, the uncorrected Brownian approximation is unreliable because it ignores the overshoot of \Lambda_k past the boundary. Siegmund's corrected diffusion approximations incorporate the overshoot and are typically as good as, and often better than, large-deviation approximations for linear boundaries, including truncated sequential probability ratio tests [1†L11-L15][1†L48-L51]. The boundary-crossing surface is therefore computed with Siegmund-corrected approximations or exact recursions where feasible, and the uncorrected Brownian approximation is reported only as a screening quantity, never as the design object.

D.3 Reporting convention

For each test statistic (SPRT, CUSUM, Shiryaev–Roberts), the architecture reports the triple (P_{\mathrm{det}}(T_{\mathrm{dec}}),\, \alpha_{\mathrm{actual}},\, \mathbb{E}[\tau \mid H_1]) at the pre-specified operating point, with the approximation method labelled. Comparisons across test statistics are made at matched \alpha_{\mathrm{actual}}, not at matched nominal \alpha, because the truncation shifts the two apart.

