Appendix E — The TOST Equivalence Protocol

E.1 Why the Version 0.4 test was wrong

Version 0.4 stated H_0^{\mathrm{equiv}}: P_{\mathrm{det}}^{\mathrm{PS}} - P_{\mathrm{det}}^{\mathrm{best}} \le \Delta. This is a superiority null: rejecting it establishes that PLANET-SCAN is better by more than \Delta, but failing to reject it does not establish equivalence, because non-rejection is consistent with PLANET-SCAN being substantially worse. The interpretation of a “negative result” in Version 0.4 was therefore formally invalid.

E.2 The corrected protocol

The equivalence null is

H_0^{\mathrm{TOST}}: \left| P_{\mathrm{det}}^{\mathrm{PS}}(T_{\mathrm{dec}}) - P_{\mathrm{det}}^{\mathrm{comp}}(T_{\mathrm{dec}}) \right| \ge \Delta
\quad \text{versus} \quad
H_1^{\mathrm{TOST}}: \left| P_{\mathrm{det}}^{\mathrm{PS}} - P_{\mathrm{det}}^{\mathrm{comp}} \right| < \Delta,

tested by two one-sided tests at level \alpha_{\mathrm{equiv}} on each side. Equivalence within \Delta is established only if both one-sided nulls are rejected. Failure to reject either one-sided null does not establish equivalence and is reported as inconclusive, not as a negative result for the architecture. The margin \Delta is pre-specified from mission risk tolerance; it is not tuned to the data.

E.3 Pre-specified comparator and matched \alpha

The primary comparator is pre-specified as the transformer-based telemetry-only detector (HPM-Former, or an equivalent health-state baseline model trained on nominal telemetry) [4†L8-L13][11†L14-L28]. SADDLE is retained as a secondary comparator [5†L8-L17][12†L10-L14]. The “best single layer” of Version 0.4 is demoted to a secondary comparison because post-hoc selection injects winner's-curse bias into the primary claim. All comparisons are made at matched \alpha_{\mathrm{actual}}, because detection probabilities at different false-alarm rates are not comparable. The transformer baseline is used with two disclosed caveats: it is a health-state anomaly detector rather than a fidelity detector, and it trains partly on synthetic sustained-offset samples, both of which make it a conservative comparator for PLANET-SCAN.

