Appendix G — Adversarial-Consistency Robustness Check

G.1 Object

This appendix specifies the adversarial-consistency corruption class, explains why it is a robustness check rather than a falsification arm, and states the pass/fail criterion.

G.2 The corruption

g_k^{\mathrm{adv}} = \arg\min_{g} \; \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{det}}\!\left(g\!\left(h_{\mathrm{int}}(x_k,\theta_0) + v_k^{\mathrm{int}}\right)\right)
\quad \text{s.t.} \quad \| \mathbb{E}[g(\cdot)] - h_{\mathrm{int}}(x_k,\theta_1) \| \ge \epsilon,

where \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{det}} is the log-likelihood assigned by the telemetry-only detector. The adversary is assumed to know the detector architecture.

G.3 Why it cannot falsify

A telemetry-only detector is structurally blind to this class by construction: the corruption is defined as the minimizer of that detector's likelihood ratio subject to a physical discrepancy. PLANET-SCAN will therefore “win” the comparison trivially, and a win carries no information about the architecture's added value. Conversely, if the external channel fails to detect the adversarial corruption, the architecture has failed on its home ground, and that is an alarming result, not a routine negative one. The adversarial class is therefore reported as a robustness check with a predicted win; the pass criterion is detection at the pre-specified \epsilon, and a failure is escalated rather than averaged into the falsification statistics.

G.4 Relation to the natural-class TOST

The falsification arena is the natural fault classes—sensor drift, calibration bias, true anomalies, model mismatch—where the added value of an expensive external channel is genuinely in doubt and a null result is possible. The TOST of Appendix E is run on those classes. The adversarial class is reported separately and is never used to establish superiority.

