Appendix H — Decision-Authority Hazard Decomposition

H.1 The double-counting risk

The deadline \tau \le T_{\mathrm{dec}} already constrains the stopping rule. Writing h_{\mathrm{DA}}(t) as a single hazard and multiplying the value of information by its survival function risks counting the deadline twice: once in the constraint and once in the hazard. The decomposition removes the double count.

H.2 Exogenous versus controlled

Two mechanisms close the decision option:

· Exogenous loss of optionality: fuel depletion, terrain commit points, communication blackout, aerodynamic limits. The operator does not control these.
· Decision-maker-controlled commit: the operator's own stopping boundary.

Only the exogenous component belongs in the hazard. Write

h_{\mathrm{DA}}(t) = h_{\mathrm{exo}}(t), \qquad
S_{\mathrm{exo}}(t) = \exp\!\left(-\int_0^t h_{\mathrm{exo}}(s)\,ds\right).

The controlled component is already represented in the sequential stopping rule and is not multiplied again.

H.3 Deadline-constrained value of information

\mathrm{VoI}_{\mathrm{DC}}(t) =
S_{\mathrm{exo}}(t+\delta)\,
\mathbb{E}_{z_t}\!\left[
\max_{d} \mathbb{E}[U(d,F) \mid \mathcal{F}_t, z_t]
-
\max_{d} \mathbb{E}[U(d,F) \mid \mathcal{F}_t]
\right].

The factor S_{\mathrm{exo}}(t+\delta) is the probability that the decision option survives until the observation is usable. An observation with S_{\mathrm{exo}}(t+\delta) \approx 0 has near-zero decision value even if its statistical likelihood ratio is large. This is the physical analogue of the discount factor in the Lehrer–Wang stopping framework, in which more accurate sources and higher discount factors both enlarge the waiting region and the upfront fee scheme is optimal among history-dependent schemes [13†L4-L16]. The exogenous hazard is not a discount rate; it is a physical survival probability, and its separation from the operator's commit policy is what makes the VoI expression non-circular.

